+<p>Terms introduced by Lesson 0003. Each entry: definition, then a usage sentence as you'd hear it at a desk.</p>
+<div class="scrollbox"><table>
+<tr><th>Term</th><th>Kind</th><th>Definition &amp; usage</th></tr>
+<tr><td class='term'>incremental feed</td><td class='type'>Market data</td><td>A feed that broadcasts every order-level event (add/execute/cancel/delete/replace) and expects each consumer to maintain its own book state, with no resynchronization mechanism.<br><span class='use'>“We consume the ITCH incremental feed, so a single dropped message means our book is wrong until the next session.”</span></td></tr>
+<tr><td class='term'>snapshot feed</td><td class='type'>Market data</td><td>A feed (or endpoint) that periodically publishes the current top-N book levels; consumers hold no state and self-heal on the next snapshot.<br><span class='use'>“The REST snapshot feed is fine for a dashboard, but you can&#x27;t do queue-position research on it.”</span></td></tr>
+<tr><td class='term'>event sourcing</td><td class='type'>Architecture</td><td>Deriving current state exclusively by replaying an ordered log of immutable events — the book is a fold over the message stream.<br><span class='use'>“The order book is event sourcing at nanosecond scale: state equals the fold of every message since the open.”</span></td></tr>
