+  <h1>The Cost of Observability — When the Measurement Is the Hot Path</h1>
+  <p class="why">Why this matters: adding "one cheap top-of-book read" per event turned this repo's
+  31-nanosecond book update into a 72-<em>micro</em>second one — a 2,000× slowdown that the docs had
+  even predicted. This lesson is how to find, prove, and reason about that class of cost.</p>
+</header>
+
+<h2>1 · The concept</h2>
+<p>Every signal, metric, or log you attach to a hot path is itself code with a cost, and that cost
+obeys different math than the path it observes. Three rules capture most real cases:</p>
+<ol>
+  <li><strong>Frequency multiplies everything.</strong> A read that's free when called occasionally is
+  the whole budget when called per event. The flat book's <code>top_of_book()</code> scan was designed
+  for "query rarely"; the signal engine calls it 1.5 million times a day.</li>
+  <li><strong>Complexity constants hide in domain parameters.</strong> "O(range) linear sweep over a
+  small array" was honest at 30,001 ticks (a $3 window). Nobody re-read that sentence when the range
+  became 200,000 ticks ($20). Same code, same big-O, 6.7× the constant.</li>
+  <li><strong>Prove the mechanism by varying its parameter.</strong> Suspecting the scan is a
+  hypothesis. Running the same day with two different price ranges and watching cost scale with the
+  ratio is a confirmed model: cost ratio 1.47 vs range ratio 1.48 ⇒ the scan is ~100% of the cost.</li>
+</ol>
+
+<h2>2 · Minimal standalone example</h2>
+<pre><code><span class="com"># The observer's complexity, not the system's, dominates:</span>
+<span class="kw">for</span> event <span class="kw">in</span> feed:              <span class="com"># N events</span>
+    book.apply(event)            <span class="com"># O(1)   — 31 ns measured</span>
+    snapshot = book.top()        <span class="com"># O(R)   — R = declared tick range!</span>
+    signals.append(f(snapshot))  <span class="com"># O(1)</span>
+<span class="com"># Total: O(N·R). N = 1.5e6, R = 2e5  →  3e11 slot visits ≈ your 110 seconds.</span>
+
+<span class="com"># The experiment that converts suspicion into proof:</span>
+<span class="com">#   run(R=200_000)  →  72,568 ns/event</span>
+<span class="com">#   run(R=135_000)  →  49,212 ns/event</span>
+<span class="com">#   cost ratio 1.47  ≈  range ratio 1.48  →  the O(R) term is everything.</span></code></pre>
+
+<h2>3 · In this project</h2>
+<p><span class="path">docs/perf/02-microstructure-signals.md</span> — the full write-up. Headline table:
+bare book 30.9 ns/message (synthetic bench) vs book+engine on the real AAPL day: 72,568 ns/event at
+200k ticks, 49,212 ns/event at 135k ticks. The wall-clock was 110 seconds for one symbol-day — fine
+for tonight's research, hopeless for a 500-symbol backtest, which is why the write-up specifies the
+follow-up: incremental best-index tracking in the book (O(1) reads, short repair scans), projected
+~100× improvement, deferred to its own before/after write-up.</p>
+<p><span class="path">cpp/itch/microstructure_signals.hpp</span> — <code>emit()</code> is the per-event
+observer: <code>top_of_book()</code> + OFI arithmetic + a vector push. The arithmetic is nanoseconds;
+the scan (<span class="path">cpp/itch/order_book_flat.hpp:86-105</span>) is everything else.</p>
+<p><span class="path">src/oil_alpha/microstructure/plots.py</span> — the same discipline applied to
+<em>rendering</em>: 1.5M plotly points would hang the browser, so <code>_decimate()</code> uniformly
+thins to ≤5,000 rows. Uniform decimation preserves session-scale shape (trends, regimes) and
+deliberately sacrifices single-event spikes — a documented trade-off, not an accident.</p>
+<div class="callout"><strong>Contrast with docs/perf/01:</strong> lesson 0002's flat-book story was
+"the right data structure made the book 2× faster." This chapter is its sequel and inversion: the
+same structure's convenience read, moved into a per-event loop with a 6.7× bigger domain parameter,
+became a 2,000× tax. Data structures aren't fast or slow — <em>access patterns</em> are.</div>
+
+<h2>4 · Often confused: throughput cost vs latency cost</h2>
+<div class="scrollbox"><table>
+  <tr><th></th><th>Batch/throughput view (this lesson)</th><th>Tail-latency view (lesson 0002, perf/01)</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Question</td><td>How long for 1.5M events? (wall clock, mean ns/event)</td><td>How late is the slowest 1-in-1000 event? (p99, p99.9)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Killer</td><td>Per-event work × event count (O(N·R))</td><td>Rare stalls: cache misses, allocation, OS jitter</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Fix shape</td><td>Reduce per-event complexity (incremental tracking)</td><td>Reduce variance (locality, no allocation in path)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>When each matters</td><td>Research/backtests over big files</td><td>Live trading, fleeting opportunities</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Also confused:</td><td colspan="2"><strong>Decimation vs aggregation</strong> — decimation (every k-th
+  point) preserves shape cheaply but drops extremes; aggregation (per-bucket min/max/OHLC) keeps
+  extremes at more cost. The dashboard decimates; the λ estimator aggregates (1s bars). Each choice
+  matches what its consumer needs.</td></tr>
+</table></div>
+
+<h2>5 · Practice quiz</h2>
+<p>Score: <span id="score">0 / 4</span></p>
+
+<div class="question" data-question>
+  <p>Q1. The engine run took ~110s at 200k ticks and ~74s at 135k ticks, same file. What's the strongest correct conclusion?</p>
+  <ul class="options">
+    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Cost tracked the tick range almost exactly (1.47 vs 1.48) while everything else stayed fixed — a controlled experiment isolating the O(range) scan as essentially the entire per-event cost.">The top-of-book scan dominates: per-event cost is linear in the declared tick range</li>
+    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Parsing was identical in both runs (same file, same messages); if it dominated, the times would match.">ITCH parsing dominates, since both runs parse the same bytes</li>
+    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The Python conversion is O(events), identical across runs — it can't explain a difference that tracks the tick range.">The pybind11 list conversion dominates the cost</li>
+    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Narrower bounds dropped only 15 events out of 1.5M — five orders of magnitude too small to explain a 35s difference.">The tighter bounds processed meaningfully fewer events, explaining the speedup</li>
+  </ul>
+  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
+</div>
+
+<div class="question" data-question>
+  <p>Q2. perf/01 truthfully called the best-bid scan "cache-friendly and fast." perf/02 truthfully blames it for a 2,000× tax. What changed?</p>
+  <ul class="options">
+    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Same machine family, same compiler class — and no hardware change turns 31ns into 72µs.">The hardware — perf/01 ran on a faster machine</li>
+    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Two domain parameters moved: the scan went from occasional (benchmark queries) to per-event (1.5M calls), and the range from 30k to 200k ticks. Frequency × constant is what exploded; the code and its big-O never changed.">The access pattern (rare → per-event) and the domain constant (30k → 200k ticks) — not the code</li>
+    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The book's update path is untouched and still ~31ns; the cost lives in the new caller, not a regression.">A regression: the X-cancel fix accidentally slowed the book down</li>
+    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Real messages parse at the same per-byte cost as synthetic ones; data realism changes correctness exposure, not scan arithmetic.">Real data is inherently slower to process than synthetic data</li>
+  </ul>
+  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
+</div>
+
+<div class="question" data-question>
+  <p>Q3. Why does <code>plots.py</code> decimate to 5,000 points instead of plotting all 1.5M — and what does that choice knowingly sacrifice?</p>
+  <ul class="options">
+    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Plotly renders what it's given — the browser hangs, but no library-imposed cap silently truncates at 5k.">Plotly refuses to render more than 5,000 points</li>
+    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Rendering cost is the observability cost of the GUI; uniform thinning keeps session-scale shape interactive while knowingly dropping single-event spikes — documented as a trade-off, with aggregation (like the λ estimator's 1s bars) as the alternative when extremes matter.">Interactive rendering needs bounded points; uniform decimation keeps the shape but knowingly drops single-event extremes</li>
+    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Decimation happens after the events exist — it saves rendering, not parsing.">It makes the C++ parse faster by skipping events</li>
+    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Statistical validity lives in the estimator (which uses ALL events via 1s bars); plots are diagnostics, and thinning them loses detail, not validity.">5,000 is the minimum sample size for statistical validity</li>
+  </ul>
+  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
+</div>
+
+<div class="question" data-question>
+  <p>Q4. The proposed fix is incremental best-index tracking. Why did the write-up defer it instead of just doing it?</p>
+  <ul class="options">
+    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The projection is ~100×; the fix is emphatically worth doing — the question was when and how, not whether.">Because the projected gain is too small to justify the work</li>
+    <li data-correct="true" data-why="It mutates book internals shared with the calibrated latency benchmarks (perf/01's before/after numbers), so it deserves its own measured before/after write-up rather than riding along in a dashboard PR — changes to measured hot paths get measured in isolation.">Because it changes book internals shared with the latency benchmarks, so it must land as its own measured change</li>
+    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The repair-scan design is described in the write-up; the approach is known, just not yet implemented and measured.">Because nobody knows how to implement it yet</li>
+    <li data-correct="false" data-why="110s is painful even for research and prohibitive for multi-symbol work — the cost is real; discipline, not indifference, deferred it.">Because 110 seconds per day is acceptable, so the cost doesn't matter</li>
+  </ul>
+  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
+</div>
+
+<h2>6 · Check your understanding</h2>
+<p><strong>Scenario:</strong> You want to log the full 10-level book depth on every event "for later
+research." Using this lesson's three rules, what do you check before shipping it, and what's the
+likely verdict? <em>(Frequency: 1.5M events × the depth-extraction cost — if extraction scans levels,
+you've rebuilt the O(N·R) trap with N·10R. Domain constants: how wide is a level walk? Proof: measure
+with logging on/off, and vary depth 10→5 to confirm scaling. Verdict: extract incrementally or
+aggregate per bar, don't scan per event.)</em></p>
+
+<script>
+(function () {
+  "use strict";
+  var right = 0;
+  var qs = document.querySelectorAll("[data-question]");
+  function shuffle(a){for(var i=a.length-1;i>0;i--){var j=Math.floor(Math.random()*(i+1));var t=a[i];a[i]=a[j];a[j]=t;}return a;}
+  qs.forEach(function (q) {
+    var list = q.querySelector(".options");
+    shuffle(Array.prototype.slice.call(list.children)).forEach(function(li){ list.appendChild(li); });
+    var fb = q.querySelector(".feedback");
+    var done = false;
+    list.querySelectorAll("li").forEach(function (li) {
+      li.addEventListener("click", function () {
+        if (done) return;
+        done = true;
+        var ok = li.dataset.correct === "true";      // attribute, never position
+        if (ok) right++;
+        li.classList.add(ok ? "right" : "wrong");
+        list.querySelectorAll("li").forEach(function (el) {
+          el.classList.add("locked");
+          if (el.dataset.correct === "true") el.classList.add("right");
+        });
+        fb.hidden = false;
+        fb.textContent = (ok ? "✓ Correct. " : "✗ Not quite. ") + li.dataset.why;
+        fb.style.color = ok ? "var(--ok)" : "var(--bad)";
+        document.getElementById("score").textContent = right + " / " + qs.length;
+      });
+    });
+  });
+})();
+</script>
+</div>
+</body>
+</html>


