+<h1>The Cost of Observability</h1>
+<p>Terms introduced by Lesson 0005. Each entry: definition, then a usage sentence as you'd hear it at a desk.</p>
+<div class="scrollbox"><table>
+<tr><th>Term</th><th>Kind</th><th>Definition &amp; usage</th></tr>
+<tr><td class='term'>observability cost</td><td class='type'>Concept</td><td>The compute cost of the measurement/signal/logging code itself, as distinct from the system being observed — it concentrates wherever it runs per event.<br><span class='use'>“The book update is 31 ns; the observability cost of reading the top every event is 72 µs.”</span></td></tr>
+<tr><td class='term'>hot path</td><td class='type'>Performance</td><td>The code executed once per event/request, where any added cost is multiplied by the full event count.<br><span class='use'>“top_of_book() moved from a benchmark query into the hot path — that&#x27;s the whole story.”</span></td></tr>
+<tr><td class='term'>domain parameter</td><td class='type'>Performance</td><td>A quantity from the problem domain (tick range, depth levels, symbol count) that silently sets the constant inside a complexity class.<br><span class='use'>“Same O(range) code; the domain parameter grew from 30k to 200k ticks and the constant ate us.”</span></td></tr>
+<tr><td class='term'>parameter-variation experiment</td><td class='type'>Method</td><td>Confirming a suspected cost mechanism by rerunning with only its parameter changed and checking the cost scales accordingly.<br><span class='use'>“Cost ratio 1.47 vs range ratio 1.48 — the parameter-variation experiment closed the case.”</span></td></tr>
+<tr><td class='term'>O(N·R) trap</td><td class='type'>Performance</td><td>A per-event O(R) operation inside an N-event loop: neither factor looks alarming alone, the product dominates the run.<br><span class='use'>“Logging 10-level depth per event is the O(N·R) trap with a bigger R.”</span></td></tr>
+<tr><td class='term'>decimation</td><td class='type'>Data reduction</td><td>Keeping every k-th point of a series: cheap shape-preserving thinning that knowingly drops single-point extremes.<br><span class='use'>“The dashboard decimates 1.5M events to 5k points — spikes are sacrificed for interactivity.”</span></td></tr>
+<tr><td class='term'>aggregation (bars)</td><td class='type'>Data reduction</td><td>Summarizing each fixed bucket (sum, close, min/max/OHLC): costlier than decimation but preserves totals and extremes for statistics.<br><span class='use'>“The λ estimator aggregates into 1-second bars, so no event is ever dropped.”</span></td></tr>
+<tr><td class='term'>incremental tracking</td><td class='type'>Optimization</td><td>Maintaining a derived value (like the best-price index) updated on each mutation instead of recomputing it by scan on each read.<br><span class='use'>“Incremental tracking of best bid/ask turns the O(R) read into O(1) with short repair scans.”</span></td></tr>
+</table></div>
